        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> Before 0.12, we put the repos in `/usr/local/share/nvim/site/pack/plugins/start`. Am I correct in thinking that it's just not possible to tell `vim.pack` to 'pick up' such existing repos with a simlink like this:\n> \n> $ export XDG_DATA_HOME=\"/usr/local/share\"\n> $ ln -s /usr/local/share/nvim/site/pack/plugins/start /usr/local/share/nvim/site/pack/core/opt\n> \n> and that the only way to migrate is to remove the old repos and write the relevant GitHub links in vim.lua?\n\nManaging plugins with `vim.pack` also implies that they are installed via`vim.pack`. It *might* be possible to link the existing plugin package directory to the one used by `vim.pack`, but there will be no guarantees about how stable this works. Probably not a good idea for a corporate sysadmin type of scenario.\n\nI'd also point out that `pack/*/start` and `pack/*/opt` are very different plugins package directories. The first one is autoloaded on startup, while the second one requires an explicit `:packadd`. So even if symlink works, some changes to `init.lua` would still be needed.\n\nAnd yes, the plain plugin packages will continue to work, or course. So there is no hard requirement to migrate to `vim.pack`.",
            "created_at": "2026-09-19T08:06:26Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/41974#issuecomment-5740376612",
            "id": 5740376612,
